editorial:
## Method and reasoning
At time x, a machine has finished floor(x/p) items. Feasibility is monotone, so search for the first feasible time. The fastest machine alone gives an upper bound of min(p) × T. Stop adding once the total reaches T to avoid accumulating a very large sum.

## Walk through the sample
At time 7, machines with periods 3, 2, 5 have produced 2 + 3 + 1 = 6 items. At time 8 the count becomes 2 + 4 + 1 = 7. These adjacent times certify the minimum.

## Complexity
Time O(n log(min(p) × T)), space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    long long target;
    cin >> n >> target;
    vector<long long> period(n);
    for (auto &p : period)
        cin >> p;
    long long l = 1, r = *min_element(period.begin(), period.end()) * target;
    while (l < r) {
        long long mid = l + (r - l) / 2, made = 0;
        for (long long p : period) {
            made += mid / p;
            if (made >= target)
                break;
        }
        if (made >= target)
            r = mid;
        else
            l = mid + 1;
    }
    cout << l << '\n';
}
